  "name": "Bend-skin (Douala drums-and-maracas street song)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Urban popular music of Douala that emerged in the 1990s and shares its name with the city's motorcycle taxis. It grew out of tchamassi, the Bamileke dance rhythm that André-Marie Tala had popularised, and is played on drums and maracas under a vocalist who both sings and raps; Kouchouam Mbada is its best-known group. A generic shaker card stands in for the maracas.",
  "recipe": "classic mid-1990s bend-skin, groove Percussion, central east south African branch. single leader group response. acoustic membrane drum, shaker source unspecified. urban courtyard. ORTF stereo pair, portable field-recorder pre. 24-bit digital.",
